// The crowd solver in the configuration the game actually has.
//
// It exists because of a surprise: `shoving_through_the_horde` walks due +X
// through the same 64 bodies and produces **zero** crowd contacts — its golden
// trace did not change by a line when enemy-enemy separation landed. The horde
// spawns on an axis-aligned grid 0.7 apart against a 0.5 contact distance, so
// pushing along an axis slides bodies down the corridors between rows and never
// closes the 0.2 of slack. Push across the grid and 20 of 60 ticks report
// contacts.
//
// The lesson is about the test, not the solver: a horde laid out on a grid has
// an axis you can travel along and see nothing.
(
    description: "Shoved across its own grid the horde compresses, where shoved along it, it does not.",
    setup: (enemies: 64),
    inputs: [
        // Normalised on the way in, so this is a full-speed diagonal rather
        // than root-two times the speed.
        (at: 0, ticks: 60, dir: (1.0, -1.0)),
    ],
    budget: (ticks: 60),
    expect: (
        enemy_count: 64,
        trace: "shoving_diagonally_jostles_the_crowd.trace",
    ),
)
